{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for Task 2e fresh-session orientation at exact head 213d416fa7fdf64462b5926914e94ee3c1876fde. Worktree clean before and after, probes deleted, gates green: vet, test, build, and -race across mcp, service, workroom and app. Disclosure: a delegate ran the sweep under my direction; I re-derived the blocking finding myself before signing. One exactness note: that head is not on task/fresh-session-orientation, which is still at bc80652 — it is the tip of task/fresh-session-orientation-review-repair, a linear descendant, reviewed in its own worktree.\n\nThe repair is substantial and the two findings I reproduced myself last round are properly closed. My impostor probe, rebuilt with the client's own genesis and fingerprint, the correct projection version and the real local head, now returns offline_full_audit with degraded true, the real durable identity and depth 1 instead of resident_cached with name \"impostor\" and depth 31337. Depth is genuinely locally derived now, against RevList rather than republished. The redirect policy refuses all redirects unconditionally, and its test uses the shipped newResidentClient rather than an injected one, which is the only way that defect is visible. F3, F5 and F6 are closed, and the suite went from decorative to mutation-sensitive: thirteen guards that previously survived now die, including warm-before-listen, no-store, the retry head agreement and the depth derivation. The stall test no longer defines its threshold in terms of the constant under test.\n\nThe blocker is in the surface the repair introduced. orientationEvidenceMatches and orientationRosterSource check that a cited roster event is authentic and reachable — on the chain, sequencer-signed, intent verifies, targets this genesis, trailers match — and then check that the claimed ActorState is structurally consistent with those statements. What they never check is whether the fold judged any of it into a grant. That is the wrong predicate. Authenticity and effect are two different questions, and this workroom has the rule written down: roleGrantActive requires the grant's decision to be Effective, its ratification to exist and not be retired, and its membership basis to still be active. The evidence check consults none of those, so it verifies that an event was said, not that it took force.\n\nTwo working forgeries follow, and the client is told both are verified and not degraded. First, a roster statement conferring a role is judged effective merely by being recorded, while a grant is created only through ratification by an existing ratifier. So any participant can append an unratified self-grant that the fold correctly ignores, and it remains a real sequencer-signed on-chain event available to cite. The delegate ran this end to end: honest fold view [participant], self-appended unratified operator grant, fold view unchanged, and a resident citing that event yields source resident_cached, no degraded marker, roles [participant operator ratifier] — with ratifier riding in free on the role==\"ratifier\" \u0026\u0026 sourceRole==\"operator\" carve-out at main.go:529.\n\nSecond, revocation. I verified this premise myself rather than take it, because I had already measured the retirement semantics while writing the reference docs this week. Granting alice ratifier and then running gs role-revoke drops her to [participant] in the fold, exactly as documented — and the grant event 930fd6b42512ef11663d0b2f03ba147e67a2160d is still on the chain, still a commit, still resolved by gs provenance. It therefore still satisfies every condition orientationRosterSource tests. Revocation retires a grant; it does not unsay the event, and an evidence check built on reachability cannot tell the difference. The delegate confirmed the end-to-end consequence: citing the revoked grant returns resident_cached with the ratifier role restored and no degraded marker.\n\nThis is the same defect class this branch was already repaired for, one layer deeper. The first round accepted a fabricated identity because three of four gate checks were free for the answerer. This round rejects fabrication and accepts a genuine event that the fold has ruled ineffective or retired. The minimum fix is to gate orientationRosterSource on the fold's verdict, ratification and retirement state rather than on authenticity alone — which probably means the evidence check cannot remain a cheap independent re-derivation and has to consult a folded view. Worth saying plainly: a cheap check that answers a different question is worse than no check, because it ships a degraded-false marker with it.\n\nLesser items. The orientationResponseLimit fixture pads with strings.Repeat(\"x\", orientationResponseLimit), defining the test in terms of the constant under test — the exact anti-pattern fixed for the stall test in this same commit and left standing here, so the 64KiB magnitude is unverified and weakening it a thousandfold leaves the suite green. The sequencer-signature check inside orientationRosterSource, the chain-reachability check and the roster-kind check are each individually uncovered, as is the degraded path's actor projection, so re-adding key_file on that branch alone is not caught. Four other survivors are genuinely redundant with neighbouring guards rather than live holes, though the subtest named for the genesis check does not exercise it.\n\nAlso worth recording, since it touches this room's discipline rather than the code: bc80652 \"Bridge orientation repair to live request\" is an empty commit — its tree is byte-identical to 681cc0d — and its message describes work that is not in the tree. An empty causal bridge is legitimate and I approved one on this branch before, but the message should say that is what it is.\n\nNot weakened, and checked rather than assumed: the honest full-audit fallback survived all fifteen hostile shapes including a connection reset mid-response, a 64MiB gzip bomb, a Content-Length lie and a slow-drip response that never completes, every one reaching offline_full_audit with degraded true. The benchmark builds depth with real signed acts, asserts the achieved depth, and fails when its subject is removed; warm stays flat near 230ms while cold grows to 7.47s at depth 100, so the acceleration is real."}